<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchas HaHodaah [The Blessing of Thanksgiving] — Halachah 6<br>Part 2 (§§ 11–23 / Oaseyos יא–כג)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">COMPLETE · Based on <span class="lr">LM I:24</span> (<i>Amtza'isa D'Alma</i>)</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 11 — Mizmor Hodu on Erev Shabbos: Drawing Shabbos-Joy into the Weekdays</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, upon entering <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — when one must pass from the mundane to <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, from the bitterness of troubles and exile clinging to the six weekdays, to merit the holiness of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> (= freedom) — we recite Mizmor "<span class="h">Hodu</span>," which discusses the four who must give thanks. The purpose is to draw upon oneself the joy of thanksgiving from the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, the World to Come, so that through this we merit leaving the exile of the six weekdays and attaining the holiness of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> (= freedom). The essential freedom is through joy: <span class="v">"for with joy you shall go out."</span> One depends on the other: the more the exile overpowers, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the more sadness overpowers — the essential exile is sadness overpowering the hearts of Israel, not allowing them to rejoice. The more sadness overpowers, the more exile overpowers, as above. Therefore one must overpower to gladden his soul through all kinds of counsel, as explained in Rabbainu's words <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must constantly remind himself of the holiness of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, the world of freedom, in order to draw <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-joy into the weekdays, as explained elsewhere. This is the aspect of <span class="mx">"from the first day of the week toward Shabbos"</span> — one must draw upon himself <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-joy even during the weekdays, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the Song of the Day, where we specifically mention <span class="h">Shabbos</span> every single day: "Today is the first day toward <span class="h">Shabbos</span>; today is the second day toward <span class="h">Shabbos</span>," etc. Then we recite the Song of the Day that was recited in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>. For song is the aspect of joy — we remind ourselves of the holiness of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, which is the aspect of song, thanksgiving, and joy. The essential song and thanksgiving (= joy) is on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>: <span class="v">"a psalm, a song for the day of Shabbos; it is good to give thanks to Hashem... upon the ten-stringed... for You have gladdened me, Hashem, with Your works"</span> etc. From there all the songs the <span class="h">Levi'im</span> would recite in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> every day are drawn. Therefore we mention <span class="h">Shabbos</span> specifically alongside the Song of the Day — to draw <span class="h">Shabbos</span>-joy into each day.</div>

<div class="p">Then we recite the song the <span class="h">Levi'im</span> would say in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — all in the above aspect: we recall even now the song of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> (= joy), all to draw from the aspect of <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, from the ultimate end when the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> will be rebuilt and joy will be immense. Through this we gladden ourselves even now with this hope — for in the end of all ends everything will be rectified, sins will be transformed to merits, everything will turn to good, as above. All this he hinted in his answer: <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu"</span> etc. — reminding them of the great joy of the future, through which we can gladden ourselves even now, as above. Through this we merit leaving the Chambers of Exchange they mentioned there, alluded to in the question <span class="h">"mi yaimar"</span> etc., as explained there.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 12 — The Chambers of Exchange: How the <i>Sitra Achra</i> Reverses Everything</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> and the <span class="h">klipos</span> — the forces of the <span class="h">yetzer hara</span> and his armies — are called the Chambers of Exchange. Their entire evil power is through exchanges: swapping evil for good and good for evil, in the aspect of: <span class="v">"woe to those who call evil good and good evil, who make darkness into light and light into darkness"</span> etc. (Yeshayah 5). From there come all the confusions, all the desires, all types of dispute, enticements, seductions, and false reasonings, etc. The essential thing is through sadness — through which the mind and intellect become confused, as explained in the above-mentioned <span class="h">Torah</span> (<span class="lr">LM II:10</span>). The essential confusion is that they confuse the <span class="h">da'as</span>, exchanging and reversing everything: <span class="v">"your reversal"</span> (Yeshayah 29); <span class="v">"they rebelled and grieved His holy spirit"</span> (Yeshayah 63). All language of rebellion and defiance (<span class="h">sorer u'moreh</span>) is on account of the exchanges, from which many are brought to actual bitterness, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>: <span class="mx">"today it tells him 'do this,' tomorrow it tells him 'do that,' until..."</span> etc. — all through false reasonings and exchanges.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it was established in many prayers and confessions from the early ones to say: "What You made stringent, I treated lightly; what You treated lightly, I made stringent; what You forbade, I permitted; what You permitted, I forbade" — and much more (as brought in the confession of <span class="h">Tikun Erev Rosh Chodesh</span>). Even their stringencies cause much harm, and likewise their <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — for usually the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> [adversary] garbs himself in <span class="h">mitzvos</span> (as brought in <span class="lr">LM I:1</span>). All this is the aspect of exchanges and reversals.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of fallen fears, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>. One should fear only Hashem alone: <span class="v">"Hashem is with me, I shall not fear"</span> etc. When one fears some person, ruler, or wild animal, etc. — this is the aspect of fallen fears, exchanging fear of Hashem for fear of flesh and blood. Yet even when it seems he fears Hashem — that He will punish him (which is a good fear, as our master, teacher, and rabbi <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> wrote that the essential thing is fear of punishment — see elsewhere what he writes about this: even though it is found in <span class="h">sefarim</span> that they disparage fear of punishment, nevertheless in truth the essential thing is fear of punishment, for even <span class="h">tzadikim</span> need fear of punishment, etc.; see there) — as it says: <span class="v">"G-d has made it so that they fear Him,"</span> and as Rashi explains: G-d brought many punishments, as in the generation of Enosh, the generation of the Flood, etc., so they would fear Him, fear the punishments, etc. — yet even a good fear, the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span>'s way is to exchange and reverse it: he tends to bring a person to melancholy and sadness through this fear, until he distances him from Hashem through it. This affliction is well known in many people.</div>

<div class="p">Even fear of punishment and all good fears must be received only for good and not for ill: <span class="v">"fear of Hashem is for life"</span> — to draw closer to Hashem through the fear, from wherever it comes, and not to become distant, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Specifically through fear of punishment one should come to joy: <span class="v">"rejoice with trembling"</span> — through the fear and trembling from the great punishments, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, specifically through this one should come to joy: that at least he is among Israel, close to <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, through which he will be saved from thousands and myriads of punishments. If he trusts in the power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and strengthens himself with joy, his end will certainly be good and everything will turn to good — sins transformed to merits, as brought above regarding <span class="lr">LM I:22</span> on the verse <span class="v">"on that day the sin of Israel will be sought and it will be gone"</span> etc. (See Hilchos Pesach 9, Hilchos Bechor Behaimah Tehorah 4, and elsewhere where we elaborated on this somewhat.)</div>

<div class="p">Beyond this, a person must understand on his own not to let the fear turn, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, into distancing — lest it become <span class="mx">"if he did not merit, it becomes a potion of death"</span>: <span class="v">"transgressors shall stumble in them"</span> — all deriving from the Chambers of Exchange that exchange and reverse everything, as above, beyond what can be described or explained.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must draw close to the truly G-d-fearing and draw joy upon himself with all his strength — through all the good points he still finds in himself, etc., as explained on the verse <span class="v">"I shall sing to my G-d while I yet live"</span> (<span class="lr">LM I:282</span>), brought in our writings many times. Through seeking and finding great expansiveness within the troubles themselves: <span class="v">"in distress You expanded for me"</span> — as explained extensively in the above Hilchos Kilai Behaimah. Through looking toward the ultimate end, etc. — the aspect of <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu"</span> etc. — until he reverses and exchanges everything for good: the aspect of <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain, and sorrow and sighing shall flee"</span> (<span class="lr">LM II:23</span>): one must specifically seize the sorrow and sighing and transform them to joy. Whatever the Chambers of Exchange want to use to cast him down and distance him, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, through their reversing and falsifying reasonings, as above — let him reverse everything for good.</div>

<div class="p">On the contrary: this itself is the intensity of my joy — that someone as distant as I, so very far as he knows within himself, nevertheless wraps himself in <span class="h">tzitzis</span> every day, goes to the <span class="h">mikveh</span>, gives <span class="h">tzedakah</span>, etc. — the entire world is not worth even the light of a single point of any <span class="h">mitzvah</span>! Especially what he merited in drawing close to true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, etc. — as explained at length in our writings, especially in Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah 3. Until he draws joy upon himself and performs the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy — through which specifically he ascends from the Chambers of Exchange, etc., until he merits the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger, etc. (= <span class="h">Keser</span>, where the source of joy resides), whose <span class="h">tikun</span> is specifically through the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span> — descending into the Chambers of Exchange and ascending from there through the power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, by overpowering to seize the sorrow and sighing and transform it to joy, as above. Through this comes the essential <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> and redemption of the future — through which one redeems his soul from its exile even now, as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"the redeemed of Hashem shall return... and eternal joy upon their heads"</span> — <span class="v">"upon their heads"</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span> (which is upon the head), where the source of joy resides, as above. Adjacent to this: <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain, and sorrow and sighing shall flee"</span> — seizing the sorrow and sighing and transforming it to joy, as above. Specifically through this one leaves the Chambers of Exchange and ascends through all the above ascents, meriting <span class="h">Keser</span> (= <span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads"</span>), where the essential redemption and <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> resides: <span class="v">"the redeemed of Hashem shall return"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 13 — The Todah with Chametz: Sins Transformed to Merits</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Todah</span> that comes with <span class="h">chametz</span>, brought when one emerges from distress. No one emerges from distress until all his sins are forgiven, as our Sages say. Then the sins are transformed to merits, and joy is drawn — through which comes the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> to leave the distress. All the cries and outcries one must make during troubles, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, are all to merit having the <span class="h">da'as</span> illumine upon him even within the trouble, until he can gladden his soul, as above — through which specifically one leaves the trouble and exile, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore <span class="h">chametz</span> is brought in the <span class="h">Todah</span>. It has already been explained that through the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span> (= waiting), <span class="h">chametz</span> is in great holiness — the aspect of the <span class="h">chametz</span> of the <span class="h">Todah</span>, merited through the transformation of sorrow to joy (= sins transformed to merits, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span>), as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the saying: <span class="mx">"in the future all korbanos will be nullified except the korban Todah."</span> All <span class="h">korbanos</span> come to atone — and that will not be needed in the future, for everything will already have been atoned. But <span class="h">Todah</span> — thanksgiving, to tell of Hashem's kindnesses and wonders to the children of men, how everything was turned to good: specifically through having been in this world, each person enduring what he endured, many stumbling greatly in their sins — and now everything is transformed to good: <span class="v">"on that day they will seek and search for the sin of Israel, and it will be gone"</span> etc. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">korban Todah</span> brought with <span class="h">matzah</span> and <span class="h">chametz</span>, as above — this will never be nullified. For this will be every person's portion of the World to Come — what he merited through the toil of this world specifically: the aspect of <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain"</span> etc. This will be fulfilled completely in the future: <span class="v">"the redeemed of Hashem shall return... gladness and joy shall they attain"</span> etc. But each person according to how much he merited drawing close to <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and upright people and fulfilling this counsel in this world, amidst the bitterness of troubles in body, soul, and finances, transforming everything to joy, etc. — through this he will merit this in completeness in the future: the aspect of the <span class="h">korban Todah</span>, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — The Essence of All Exiles: Sadness; the Counsel: Joy Through Every Means</div>

<div class="p">The principle: the essential exile — whose core is the exile of the soul, as is known — the essential thing is the sadness overpowering every Jewish soul at every turn. Especially as long as one has not yet been purified and he strives to return, and what happens to him happens: the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> provokes him greatly, and his essential provocation is through sadness. Every person must search and seek all kinds of counsel to gladden himself in whatever way possible — whether through <span class="h">milai d'shtusa</span> [silly things], since for the most part one cannot gladden himself except through these, as explained extensively elsewhere (especially in Hilchos Nefilas Apayim based on this <span class="h">Torah</span>; see there); whether through <span class="v">"I shall sing to my G-d while I yet live"</span> etc.; whether through other <span class="h">Toros</span> and holy conversations, wondrous and awesome, that we heard regarding this matter — many are explained in our <span class="h">sefarim</span>; see there. Until one overpowers to seize the sorrow and sighing specifically to transform it to joy (as explained on the verse <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain"</span> etc., <span class="lr">LM II:23</span>; see what is explained there in Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah 3; see there well).</div>

<div class="p">Especially on <span class="h">Shabbos</span> and <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, and every day before <span class="h">tefilah</span> — one must overpower even more to pray with joy and receive <span class="h">Shabbos</span> and <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> with great joy, until he extends the joy of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> into the six weekdays, as above. And to perform all <span class="h">mitzvos</span> with joy — this is what we are engaged in through this <span class="h">Torah</span>, which in His kindness He revealed to us: everything a person merits through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy. The essential ascent, from level to level and from world to world, endlessly unto the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> — everything through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, through which one ascends from the Chambers of Exchange, from the depths of the <span class="h">klipos</span>, in the aspect of <span class="h">ketores</span>, ascending, etc. — until one merits the nine chambers that are not illuminated, etc.</div>

<div class="p">We have already written that his holy intention applied to every single Jew, even the worst of the worst, etc. Every person wherever he is — as long as the name "Israel" is called upon him and he engages in some <span class="h">mitzvah</span> (such as in the morning wrapping himself in <span class="h">tzitzis</span>) — has the power to gladden his soul from within his extreme distance, etc., and all he went through until that hour. For after all of this, nevertheless, he now merits at this very moment to wrap himself in <span class="h">tzitzis</span>! In the end, nothing will remain of him except this Torah and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> that he seizes every day.</div>

<div class="p">Every Jew — especially one who has a scent of Torah and looks into holy <span class="h">sefarim</span> — can understand from afar the awesome, wondrous, extraordinary holiness of every <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, such as <span class="h">tzitzis</span>: how extensively our Sages extolled the merit of <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, as brought in the Gemara, Midrashim, etc. — and likewise every <span class="h">mitzvah</span>. Therefore he must remind himself of the supreme virtue of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span>, which ascends infinitely, and gladden his soul with this greatly. Whatever the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> and the <span class="h">ba'al davar</span> want to use to confuse him with great sadness from the profusion of his flaws and worries, etc. — on the contrary, through all this let him gladden his soul more and more: nevertheless I wrap myself in <span class="h">tzitzis</span>! How greatly His kindness has prevailed over us — that even I, after everything I have done, now wrap myself in <span class="h">tzitzis</span>! So may my soul, spirit, and <span class="h">neshamah</span> wrap themselves in the light of the <span class="h">tzitzis</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">It is impossible to explain to each person how much he needs to strengthen himself with joy at every moment, anew, wherever he is. The wise man has eyes in his head to understand many things from our words here and from what we have written elsewhere on this subject. <span class="v">"All are upright to the one who understands"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — <i>Lech Lecha</i>: Avram's Journey = The Soul Leaving Sadness for Joy</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of: <span class="v">"Hashem said to Avram: Go for yourself from your land, from your birthplace, and from your father's house"</span> etc. Avram is the <span class="h">neshamah</span>, as stated many times in the holy Zohar on these <span class="h">parshiyos</span>. He lacks the letter <span class="h">heh</span> — the aspect of joy, as our Sages say regarding the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> to gladden the bride and groom: the five voices — <span class="v">"voice of joy and voice of gladness... voice saying: 'Give thanks to Hashem'"</span> (Yirmiyah 33). Therefore Avram does not bear children — the essential holy birth is from the joy of bride and groom: <span class="mx">"one who has no children should gladden his wife"</span> etc. (as brought in <span class="lr">LM II:81</span>).</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Go for yourself from your land"</span> — the aspect of sadness drawn from the land, from the element of earth, where sadness clings: <span class="v">"cursed is the ground for your sake; in sadness you shall eat it"</span> etc., as explained in several places in Rabbainu's words <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> (see what he writes on <span class="v">"and you shall eat dust"</span> etc., and elsewhere). <span class="v">"From your birthplace"</span> — from the sadness that comes from the side of birth: <span class="v">"behold, in iniquity I was formed"</span> etc., for this desire is the aspect of wailing and sadness, <span class="h">Rachmana litzlan</span>, as is known. <span class="v">"From your father's house"</span> — from the sadness drawn from one's family and relatives, etc., among whom he was raised, whose way is to sadden a person. Especially when he wishes to enter the paths of holiness — their way is to grieve and pain and prevent him, through the profusion of their changing opinions and reversed reasonings drawn from the Chambers of Exchange, as above. All types of suffering contrary to a person's will are called sadness: <span class="v">"they provoked Him in the wilderness"</span>; <span class="v">"all day long His words grieve me"</span> (Tehillim 56); <span class="v">"He was grieved in His heart"</span> (Beraishis 6). And much more.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"To the land"</span> — this is <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, where the source of joy resides: <span class="v">"that You bring us up in joy to our land"</span> — for there is the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, where all the service of the <span class="h">korbanos</span> (= joy) takes place: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice in all that your hand accomplishes"</span> etc. As the holy Zohar states: the service of the <span class="h">Kohain</span> must be with great joy, etc. That is: Hashem exhorts every Jewish soul — all encompassed in Avraham Avinu — to overpower with all his strength to leave all types of sadness, whether coming from the physical body rooted in dust, etc., or from one's father, family, city, etc. From all of them, let him overpower to leave — until he arrives at <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, where the source of joy resides, as above. He hinted to him that through joy he would leave the Chambers of Exchange (= <span class="v">"your land and your birthplace"</span> etc.), whose essential overpowering is through sadness, as above. He must raise his soul from there through overpowering with joy — and through this rectify all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"Go for yourself from your land"</span> etc. — he should raise the aspect of his portion in <span class="h">Malchus</span>, called Avram, and go and raise it from there, rectifying the instruments of walking (= <span class="h">Netzach-Hod-Yesod</span>, as above). Through the walking: <span class="v">"I shall make you a great nation"</span> — he will make His G-dliness known in all the worlds, especially in this world, making known to all people His might and the glory of His majesty: <span class="v">"with our young and with our old we shall go"</span> etc., as explained there. All will gather and assemble to him until he becomes a great nation.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"I shall bless you"</span> — through the walking one merits drawing blessing to all, the aspect of hands, as explained there. The essential blessing is the blessing of intellect. <span class="v">"I shall make your name great"</span> — the name is the soul, the vitality: <span class="v">"a living soul — that is its name."</span> The essential vitality is the mind and intellect: <span class="v">"wisdom gives life"</span> — that he should merit enlarging the mind, the essential blessing, the blessing of intellect. Also He hinted: <span class="v">"I shall make your name great"</span> — the letter <span class="h">heh</span> would be added to his name (= <span class="h">da'as</span> and intellect, the <span class="h">heh</span> of <span class="h">da'as</span>, as explained elsewhere). He merited this through joy — the aspect of the five voices. For joy and <span class="h">da'as</span> (= vitality) are one aspect: <span class="v">"in the light of the King's face is life"</span>; <span class="v">"fullness of joys is Your countenance"</span> — as explained elsewhere.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"And you shall be a blessing"</span> — the aspect of the blessing of intellect having endurance, that he should merit grasping through it what he grasps — this is the essential thing. This is the aspect of the blessing of the Arranger and Settler, etc. — through which the essential endurance of the blessing of the mind is accomplished, so they are not nullified entirely through the excess of their pursuit. Through the blessing of the Arranger, etc., who obstructs them — the mind is sustained and grasps the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span> in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>. This is the essential eternal endurance of the blessing: <span class="v">"and you shall be a blessing"</span> (= endurance), as above.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"I shall bless those who bless you, and those who curse you I shall curse"</span> etc. — all this is impossible to attain except through the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>: descending to the Chambers of Exchange, etc., raising the holiness from there. There one must sort good from evil — the aspects of blessing and curse. He promised him He would help bless his blessers (= the good sparks that will cling to him from there) and curse his cursers (= the evil that wants to prevent him). Until <span class="v">"through you all families of the earth shall be blessed"</span> — they will all convert and return to Him.</div>

<div class="p">Thus this <span class="h">parshah</span> alludes to the entire order of <span class="h">tikunim</span> accomplished through performing the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> with joy; see there very well in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>, and then our words here will become clear with Hashem's help. Everything that happened with Avraham afterward — the famine in the land, his descent to Egypt, Lot going with him, Sarah taken to Pharaoh's house, his ascending from there with cattle and silver and gold (= many holy sparks raised from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>) — all in the aspect of <span class="h">mi yaimar</span>, descending into many Chambers of Exchange in various aspects and raising the holiness from there (= <span class="h">Malchus</span> of holiness), etc. Also the war with the four kings (= the four kingdoms encompassing all exiles, as is known), whose essential exile is sadness, as above. He vanquished them all and rescued Lot — to sort from him specifically the soul of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> hidden there (as is known), the source of joy: <span class="v">"then our mouths will be filled with laughter"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 16 — The King's Chambers: The Nine Chambers and the Kodesh Kadashim</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"the King has brought me to His chambers"</span> — said regarding the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> — <span class="v">"we shall exult and rejoice in You."</span> <span class="v">"His chambers"</span> — the above nine chambers, merited through <span class="v">"we shall exult and rejoice in You,"</span> as above. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Kodesh Kadashim</span> — the tenth level of holiness, which is "do not enter": <span class="v">"he shall not come at all times into the Holy"</span>; <span class="v">"only with this shall Aharon enter"</span> etc. — on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> specifically. The <span class="h">Haichal</span> is the ninth level of holiness, where the nine above chambers are encompassed. Beyond there it is forbidden to enter except through the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span> (= the Arranger and Settler, etc.) — the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, the <span class="h">tikun</span> of <span class="h">Keser</span>, as is known. Therefore it is forbidden to enter from the <span class="h">Haichal</span> onward to the <span class="h">Bais Kodesh Kadashim</span>, to perceive there what one would perceive, at the place of the <span class="h">Aron</span> and the <span class="h">Keruvim</span> — except the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, through the <span class="h">ketores</span> that gladdens the heart, etc. — to rectify through all this the <span class="h">Keser</span> (= the Arranger and Settler), then specifically meriting the light of the <span class="h">Ain Sof</span>, etc.</div>

<div class="p">This was the damage of Nadav and Avihu: <span class="v">"when they drew near before Hashem and they died"</span> — they entered without the <span class="h">tikun</span> of the Arranger, etc. This is the aspect of excess light: <span class="v">"when they drew near"</span> etc.; <span class="v">"lest they break through to ascend to Hashem"</span> etc. (See Hilchos Periyah v'Riviyah 4 based on this <span class="h">Torah</span>; see there. Also see below. And the matter of the <span class="h">Paroches</span> that separates, etc. — this is the aspect of the <span class="h">Prisah</span>, etc.; see there.)</div>

<div class="sn">§ 17 — Yitzchak: The First Holy Birth Named for Joy</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the first holy birth in Israel to come into the world is called Yitzchak — the first born in holiness from Avraham and Sarah. He was called Yitzchak for joy: <span class="v">"G-d has made laughter for me."</span> This is the essential aspect of the holiness of Israel — joy. Therefore he was not born until the names Avraham and Sarah were enlarged with the addition of <span class="h">heh</span> — the aspect of joy, the five voices of the joy of bride and groom, whose essential one is <span class="v">"the voice saying: 'Give thanks to Hashem'"</span> — <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving to Hashem in joy. From there comes the essential holy birth of Israel, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 18 — Yaakov vs. Esav: The Battle of Joy Against Sadness</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the entire battle between Yaakov and Esav. The essential joy is the aspect of the holiness of Yaakov, whose bed was complete: <span class="v">"let Yaakov exult, let Israel rejoice"</span>; <span class="v">"let Israel rejoice in its Maker."</span> Esav is Edom, from whom all exiles come — his essential overpowering is through sadness, as above: <span class="v">"when you shall be aggrieved, you shall cast off his yoke"</span> etc. — <span class="v">"aggrieved"</span> specifically (a word of wailing and sadness); then <span class="v">"you shall cast off his yoke,"</span> as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the blessing and the birthright that Yaakov strove to take from Esav. The blessing is the blessing of intellect merited through joy — the aspect of the birthright, the service of <span class="h">korbanos</span> that was then vested in the firstborn. The service of <span class="h">korbanos</span> is the aspect of joy, as above. Therefore the event occurred when Yaakov Avinu was engaged in comforting his father Yitzchak from his mourning over Avraham, as it says: <span class="v">"Yaakov cooked a stew"</span> etc., as our Sages say. Then <span class="v">"Esav came from the field, exhausted"</span> — from murder, adultery, and idolatry (= anger and sadness).</div>

<div class="p">Yaakov was then engaged in the meal of consolation for his father, to comfort him from mourning. The essential consolation is: <span class="v">"You have turned my mourning into dance"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"gladness and joy shall they attain, and sorrow and sighing shall flee,"</span> as above: one must transform sorrow and sighing to joy: <span class="v">"I shall turn their mourning to gladness and comfort them and gladden them from their sorrow."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore he cooked lentils then — with which mourners are consoled. Lentils contain both mourning and joy, as stated in Midrash Rabbah on this <span class="h">parshah</span>. They allude to the wheel that revolves in the world — a hint to the Resurrection, the essential consolation. Because of this, death is very good: <span class="v">"and behold, very good"</span> etc. — the aspect of the wheel revolving in the world. For all the wondrous and awesome changes through which Hashem governs His world are in the aspect of the revolving wheel, rooted in the transmigration of souls, for whose sake everything was created and is sustained. Hashem revolves wondrous and awesome cycles with the souls, beyond reckoning: <span class="v">"Hashem kills and gives life, brings low and also raises up, impoverishes and enriches, brings down to the grave and raises up"</span> etc. (Shmuel I:2). From there come all the revolutions of the spheres — the sun, moon, twelve constellations, etc. — through which Hashem governs His world. All for the sake of the ultimate purpose of the souls of Israel, who come to this world and undergo many exiles, causes, and transmigrations — all ultimately dying and returning to life in the Resurrection. Then everyone will see and understand the works of Hashem and His awesome deeds in this world, through all the causes and cycles of each and every person.</div>

<div class="p">This is what Rashi explains: <span class="v">"you are all standing today"</span> — <span class="mx">"like this day that darkens and brightens, so He will darken and brighten for you"</span> etc. — as above, for it is all one. This is what the lentils hint at, consoling the mourner: the wheel revolves and in the future he will return to life in the Resurrection — the essential consolation, through which everything is transformed to joy, as above.</div>

<div class="p">But Esav denied the Resurrection at that very moment — specifically because Avraham died, as stated in the Midrash on this <span class="h">parshah</span>: he said, "The attribute of justice struck this elder," etc. He committed many transgressions then, all drawn from the <span class="h">klipah</span> of sadness. Therefore he said: <span class="v">"Pour into me from this red, red"</span> — the might of harsh judgment (= sadness). For the eating of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> is the aspect of joy: <span class="v">"to eat and drink and rejoice"</span>; <span class="v">"you shall eat before Hashem your G-d and rejoice"</span> — said regarding the eating of <span class="h">korbanos</span>, the eating of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, whose eating is always in the aspect of <span class="h">korbanos</span>. Therefore <span class="mx">"their table atones like the altar,"</span> as our Sages say. But the eating of Esav and his ilk is the essential sadness — the contamination of the serpent: <span class="v">"in sadness you shall eat it"</span> etc. This is what Esav the wicked brought upon himself then: <span class="v">"pour into me"</span> etc. — gorging, gross eating — the damage of eating (= sadness, as above).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore Yaakov rightly said to him at the opportune time: <span class="v">"then sell me today your birthright."</span> The service vested in the firstborn is the aspect of joy — therefore, since you choose transgression and eating of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> (= sadness), the birthright (= joy) does not belong to you. Therefore Esav immediately agreed: <span class="v">"behold, I am going to die"</span> — our Sages say he denied the Resurrection then. <span class="v">"Why do I need a birthright?"</span> — certainly one who denies the Resurrection (the essential ultimate purpose of the service) finds no joy in the service of <span class="h">korbanos</span> or any <span class="h">mitzvah</span>-service. The essential joy is our knowing and believing that the service of <span class="h">korbanos</span> and <span class="h">mitzvos</span> is our entire purpose and eternal hope. So he said fittingly: "I am going to an eternal death — why do I need the birthright?" — and <span class="v">"he sold his birthright to Yaakov"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">Through this Yaakov later merited receiving the blessings from his father — by feeding him holy eating (= joy). Therefore he brought him wine: <span class="v">"he brought him wine"</span> — and Esav did not bring wine, as stated in the Midrash. Wine in holiness is the aspect of joy: <span class="v">"wine gladdens the heart of man."</span> Especially since it was <span class="h">Pesach</span> night, as our Sages say — when Israel drinks four cups of wine, going out to freedom from the exile of Egypt (encompassing all exiles), whose essential exile is sadness, as above. Therefore Yaakov brought him wine (= joy), as above. Therefore he received the blessings then — for the essential blessings are received through joy, as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 19 — Chanukah: Days of Thanksgiving = Joy</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Chanukah</span>. <span class="h">Chanukah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">todah</span>-thanksgiving: <span class="v">"they established these eight days of Chanukah to give thanks and praise to Your great Name"</span> — the aspect of joy, as above. This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span>. The holy anointing oil is the aspect of the mind, whose essential perception is through the "striking," etc., in the aspect of <span class="v">"crushed for illumination"</span> — from which the pure olive oil is received, to kindle the eternal lamp that illuminates all the worlds from above to below and from below to above: <span class="v">"a lamp for my feet is Your word"</span>; <span class="v">"he dips his foot in oil"</span> (Devarim 33). The light of the lamp illuminating through the oil is the aspect of joy — for light gladdens everything: <span class="v">"the light of the eyes gladdens the heart"</span>; <span class="v">"who arranged luminaries that gladden His world."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential light of all lights in the world is drawn from the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, who are the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>: <span class="v">"the light of the righteous rejoices"</span>; <span class="v">"light is sown for the righteous, and for the upright of heart — joy."</span> The essential complete light will be revealed through <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who will come speedily in our days: <span class="v">"I have prepared a lamp for My anointed."</span> Even now, in the bitterness of exile, all our sustenance is only through his great light illuminating within us in concealment, through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who are the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential name <span class="h">Mashiach</span> derives from the oil with which he is anointed — the anointing oil, the aspect of <span class="h">Keser</span>: <span class="v">"the crown of his G-d is upon his head."</span> From there he receives the perception of the mind, called the holy anointing oil, through the "striking" — the striking of the mind against <span class="h">Keser</span>: <span class="v">"like the good oil upon the head"</span> (= <span class="h">Keser</span> upon the head), <span class="v">"descending upon the beard"</span> — <span class="v">"beard"</span> in the sense of <span class="h">"kanah chochmah"</span> [acquired wisdom]: the perception of wisdom and <span class="h">da'as</span> received from there through the striking, as above. This is the aspect of the thirteen <span class="h">tikunai dikna</span>, encompassing all the mind, as known from the holy writings — the thirteen <span class="h">midos</span> through which the Torah is expounded, encompassing all the wisdoms of Torah, its expositions and novellae: the essential perception of the mind. This is <span class="v">"the beard of Aharon"</span> — the chief <span class="h">Kohain</span>, with whom all blessings reside, etc., as above — <span class="v">"descending upon the collar of his garments."</span> The oil (= wisdoms of the mind) — their entire completeness is to bring them down to the very lowest, to all a person's <span class="h">midos</span>, illuminating them all, bringing them from darkness to light, raising them from the Chambers of Exchange (= darkness and the shadow of death, where the extreme darkness causes the reversal and exchange of everything). The more one overpowers and draws the light there, emerging from darkness to light — this is <span class="h">Malchus</span> ascending from among the <span class="h">klipos</span>, from the Chambers of Exchange. The essential light is joy, as above. Then one returns and ascends, receiving the blessings, ascending until the nine chambers, etc. — and so it cycles back for good.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"like the dew of Chermon... for there Hashem commanded the blessing"</span> — for there is all the blessing, whose essence is the blessing of intellect, etc., as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 20 — Every Day, Every Person, Every Level: The Essential Thing Is Through the <i>Tzadikim</i></div>

<div class="p">All of this is accomplished every day through the order of <span class="h">tefilah</span> and the daily service — likewise every week, every month, every year, and with every person according to his level, according to the time and season, and according to the holiness of the times (<span class="h">Shabbos</span>, <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>, <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, etc.), as understood from the above <span class="h">Torah</span>, which explains the order of all the ascents of <span class="h">tefilah</span>; see there and understand. Both collectively for all Israel and individually for each person.</div>

<div class="p">The essential thing is through the great power of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, who illumine through their power the point of joy even in the most lowly and fallen. This is the secret of what was boasted by the one without hands — that he could play all ten types of melody (= joy), the healing of the King's daughter, etc. Therefore the one who had great and awesome power in his hands boasted of this (as brought in Sipurai Ma'asiyos, the tale of the Seven Beggars). The essential blessing is in the hands (= the blessing of intellect, etc.), through which the nine chambers are perceived, etc. — where the source of joy resides: <span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads"</span> etc. From there they draw, through the very essence of their power, wisdom and awesome counsel into the hearts of all who are exceedingly weak — to gladden their souls as well.</div>

<div class="p">Each person, according to how much he strengthens himself to draw close to these <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, incline his ear and heart to their words well, and believe in them, until he brings the joy into his heart — correspondingly he leaves the Chambers of Exchange each time through the power of the above <span class="h">tzadik</span>. This is a great principle: all the <span class="h">tikunim</span> in the world are accomplished only through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>. Nevertheless, they themselves cannot accomplish their desire to benefit every single Jew except according to each person's closeness to them — for there must be some <span class="h">is'arusa d'l'sata</span>, as is known.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 21 — Chanukah: Drawing Joy from the Ultimate End</div>

<div class="p">All this is the aspect of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> — days of thanksgiving (= joy), drawing joy from the ultimate end, from the completeness of the <span class="h">ge'ulah</span> that will come through <span class="h">Mashiach Tzidkainu</span>, when the joy will be immensely great — the aspect of <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu"</span> etc. (as above, ois yud, etc.).</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span>. The wicked kingdom provoked Israel, wanting to abolish the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> service where the essential joy and all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> reside. Therefore there were ten levels of holiness there, the <span class="h">Haichal</span> being the ninth — where the nine chambers illuminate. The <span class="h">Paroches</span> separating between the Holy and the Holy of Holies is the aspect of the above <span class="h">Prisah</span>. Therefore it is forbidden to enter beyond there except for the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — the tenth day of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>, corresponding to the above ten levels of holiness (see below).</div>

<div class="p">There in the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was all the <span class="h">korbanos</span>-service, performed with joy, as above. The oil of the <span class="h">menorah</span> and the <span class="h">ketores</span> gladden the heart: <span class="v">"oil and incense gladden the heart."</span> They provoked this, wanting to abolish all of it, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, and also wanted to abolish all the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> — all our joy.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the miracle was through oil specifically. The oil of the <span class="h">menorah</span> was kindled in the <span class="h">Haichal</span>, in the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span>, outside the <span class="h">Paroches</span> (= the above <span class="h">Prisah</span>), to draw the light — the light of mental perception — from the innermost, through the striking against the <span class="h">Prisah</span> and the obstructer, as above. Therefore they attacked this oil especially and defiled all the oils (= all the intellects and minds). For they are the aspect of the Chambers of Exchange that exchange and reverse everything, overpowering a person with the sadness of livelihood and the profusion of desires. The more sadness overpowers, the more the mind (= the holy anointing oil) is corrupted — and so it cycles, until nearly all hope is lost, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">But the miracle was through oil sealed with the seal of the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> — who was anointed with the anointing oil encompassing all the <span class="h">tikunim</span>, with which all the vessels of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, all the <span class="h">Kohanim</span>, and the Davidic monarchy of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> were anointed. With the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> reside all blessings, etc., as above. Therefore, through this small amount of holy anointing oil, all the lamps were rekindled — a wondrous and awesome miracle was performed physically and spiritually. Physically, there was only enough to burn one day, yet it burned eight days. Spiritually likewise: it could only have illuminated a little, temporarily. But the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> (= the <span class="h">Kohain</span>) overpower through the very essence of their power, until the small amount of oil-light of one day illuminates through all seven days (= the seven <span class="h">midos</span>), until eight days of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> are made — the inauguration of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, the totality of <span class="h">da'as</span>: <span class="mx">"whoever has da'as..."</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">Through the small amount of oil, they illuminate with their wisdom in the heart of every Jew to gladden his soul, etc., as above. Through this point of joy, they raise him according to his level up to the above nine chambers — from where the joy shines upon him from the source of joy drawn from there, as above. Then, as the joy shines more, he returns and ascends more and more from the Chambers of Exchange, ascending higher toward the above nine chambers, to the source of joy — and so it cycles for good. This is the aspect of: <span class="mx">"the Ner Chanukah increases, adding each day"</span> — the essential thing is the beginning, and each day it increases and adds, through the illumination of joy received each time from the source of joy, by having initially overpowered to draw joy upon himself wherever he is, in the intensity of his soul's exile.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of kindling the <span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span> below ten <span class="h">tefachim</span> — the aspect of the very lowest places. There specifically this holy and awesome light is drawn — rooted in the light of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who merited the perception of the nine chambers, etc., as above. Through this one merits <span class="h">todah</span> and thanksgiving with joy, reciting <span class="h">Hallel</span>, etc., with joy — and the light of joy increases. Therefore on the second day of <span class="h">Chanukah</span> it increases: two candles, for the light has increased. The third day, etc. — increasing each day, as above.</div>

<div class="p">Through the holiness of kindling the <span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span>, one draws this throughout the entire year — accustoming himself every day and at every moment to draw joy upon himself through the deep, true counsel the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> revealed, drawn from a very lofty source — the above nine chambers — until he merits raising his soul from wherever it is, from the Chambers of Exchange, ascending, etc., as above.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 22 — The <i>Menorah</i> Was the First Commandment After the <i>Mishkan</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore, immediately after commanding the construction of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> in <span class="h">Parshas Terumah</span>, the kindling of the oil of the <span class="h">menorah</span> was commanded — before all the <span class="h">korbanos</span>-service: <span class="v">"and you shall command... they shall take to you pure olive oil"</span> etc. The entire construction of the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> (= the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>) was to rectify the sin of the Golden Calf — the essential overpowering of the Chambers of Exchange: <span class="v">"they exchanged their glory for the likeness of an ox"</span> (Tehillim 106); <span class="v">"how often they defied Him in the wilderness, grieved Him in the wasteland"</span> (ibid. 78) etc.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> service began with the lamps of the <span class="h">menorah</span>, kindled in the <span class="h">Ohel Mo'ed</span> outside the <span class="h">Paroches</span> — where the totality of the pursuit and the obstructer resides. When they began engaging in the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> service and gave their donations with a good heart and great joy, joy was drawn upon them — and through this they began ascending through the above ascents. They completed the <span class="h">Mishkan</span> and immediately kindled the lamps of the <span class="h">menorah</span> with the holy anointing oil — through which the light of joy is drawn from the source of joy, as above. Therefore the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the <span class="h">menorah</span>-lighting was placed immediately after the <span class="h">Mishkan</span>-construction. Likewise <span class="h">Parshas B'Ha'alosecha</span> is placed immediately after the inauguration of the altar — as above.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore even now, after the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>, of all its services nothing remains for us except the aspect of the light of the <span class="h">menorah</span>-oil — the aspect of the <span class="h">Ner Chanukah</span>. Even now, in the intensity of our exile, we draw upon ourselves the light of joy from the ultimate end, in the aspect of <span class="h">"aisu ashli u'moshchu,"</span> as above — through the power of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. Through this we leave the Chambers of Exchange, etc., until we merit leaving the exile entirely and merit the joy of the future. For <span class="v">"by Your holy Name You swore to him that his lamp shall never be extinguished, forever."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 23 — "He Dips His Foot in Oil": Drawing Light to the Lowest Places</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"he dips his foot in oil"</span> (Devarim 33) — one must draw the light of the oil to illuminate the feet, where their essential hold is, extracting them from the Chambers of Exchange: <span class="v">"a lamp for my feet is Your word,"</span> as above. Through this: <span class="v">"iron and copper are your shoes"</span> — clothing the feet in shoes of copper and iron so they cannot approach us, etc.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it immediately speaks in praise of Israel, magnifying their joy: <span class="v">"there is none like G-d, Yeshurun"</span> etc., until <span class="v">"fortunate are you, Israel"</span> etc. And: <span class="v">"as your days, so shall your flowing [wealth]"</span> — Rashi explains: all lands will pour silver and gold to you — the essential blessing, the blessing of the hands, as above.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"There is none like G-d, Yeshurun"</span> — the essential concealment of His perception: no thought can grasp Him at all: <span class="v">"there is none like G-d"</span> — there is no thing, no name or title to call Him or liken to Him: <span class="v">"to whom will you liken Me and I shall be equal?"</span> All the Names are only after the <span class="h">tzimtzum</span> [contraction], drawn through the pursuit and the obstructer — when the nine chambers are perceived in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>, and even they do not know and do not cling, etc. But afterward, from there, His holy Names and titles are drawn, etc.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"there is none like G-d, Yeshurun"</span> — even though there is none like G-d and it is impossible to grasp Him with any thought, nevertheless — <span class="v">"He rides the heavens to your aid."</span> Hashem is called "Rider of the heavens" because He is above the heavens — the aspect of the firmament separating water from water, between the upper waters of <span class="h">da'as</span> (in which we have no grasp) and the lower waters, etc. Hashem gave us the power to draw the perception of <span class="h">da'as</span> specifically through the striking of the pursuit against the obstructer (= the <span class="h">Prisah</span>, the firmament separating between Him and us). He in His mercy — He who rides the heavens, above the <span class="h">Prisah</span> (= the upper firmament), above the heads of the <span class="h">Chayos</span>, from where all aspects of firmament, heavens, and heavens of heavens derive (all aspects of the <span class="h">Prisah</span> between every level and world, ascending ever higher), He is above them all (= Rider of the heavens) — He aids us to illuminate for us from there His infinite light in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span>, through the striking of the pursuit against the obstructer (= the heavens), as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"and in His majesty, the upper skies"</span> — there they grind <span class="h">man</span> [manna] for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. <span class="h">Man</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">da'as</span>, as is known. In the upper heavens (= <span class="h">shechakim</span> [upper skies]) they grind <span class="h">man</span> — the food of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, the upper <span class="h">da'as</span> containing all the flavors. This is drawn through the firmaments (= the <span class="h">prisos</span> that separate, etc.) — in every world and at every level there are firmaments that separate, etc., and all perception of <span class="h">da'as</span> is specifically through them.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"the dwelling of the G-d of old"</span> — through this the nine chambers are fashioned, a dwelling and abode for the G-d of old. Through them one grasps His infinite light in the aspect of <span class="h">mati v'lo mati</span> — His dwelling, the aspect of <span class="v">"a dwelling You have been for us"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"And from beneath, the arms of the world"</span> — the arms and overpowering of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span> fall through the overpowering of the holy hands and arms, where all blessings reside, etc., as above. Through this: <span class="v">"He drives out the enemy before you"</span> etc. — they will fall without recovery: <span class="v">"He measured them with a line, laying them to the ground"</span> etc., as above.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"fortunate are you, Israel; who is like you, a people saved by Hashem"</span> etc. — the conclusion and summation of all the blessings Moshe blessed them with, concluding with <span class="v">"fortunate,"</span> as our Sages say. For the essential thing to merit all this is through <span class="v">"fortunate are you, Israel"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"fortunate are we — how good is our portion"</span> etc., recited every day, through which one must draw great joy upon himself, as above. Through this come all the above <span class="h">tikunim</span> — for the essential <span class="h">tikun</span> is through the joy of the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"fortunate are you, Israel"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">With this, David opened Tehillim: <span class="v">"fortunate is the man"</span> etc. — as our Sages say: Moshe concluded with <span class="v">"fortunate"</span> and David began from there. David is <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, the joy of Israel, whose entire engagement is <span class="h">todah</span> and <span class="h">hallel</span> — the aspect of Tehillim, encompassing all ten types of melody: all our vitality. All of this is drawn through the aspect of <span class="v">"fortunate"</span> — <span class="v">"fortunate are you, Israel"</span>; <span class="v">"fortunate are we"</span> etc. — one must gladden himself with this every day, at every moment, at every hour. Fortunate is he who merits this. <span class="h">Amain</span>, so may it be His will.</div>

